  <h2>LEARNING  THEORIES </h2>
  <h3>Definition </h3>
  <p>A learning theory explains how people acquire knowledge, skills, attitudes, or behaviours</p>
  <h2>Why it Matters</h2>
  <p>It helps teachers choose teaching strategies, support learners, and assess progress.</p>
  <h2>Important point</h2>
  <p>The three theories focus on different aspects of learning; teachers can combine them.</p>
  <h1>The three theories at a glance</h1>
  <ol>Behaviourism — behaviour</ol>
  <ol>Cognitivism — thinking</ol>
  <ol>Constructivism — building understanding</ol>
  <h2>Behaviourism — behaviour</h2>
  <p>Learning is understood through observable behaviour, practice, stimuli, and consequences.</p>
  <h2>Cognitivism — thinking</h2>
  <p>Learning involves attention, memory, understanding, reasoning, and information processing.</p>
  <h2>Constructivism — building understanding</h2>
  <p>Learners actively develop knowledge through experience, reflection, and interaction.</p>
  <h1>1.Behaviourism: meaning and key ideas</h1>
  <h3>Meaning</h3>
  <p>Behaviourism explains learning as a change in observable behaviour influenced by experience and the environment.</p>
  <h3>Key Terms</h3>
  <ol>Stimulus: </ol>
  <p>something that prompts a response. Response: the learner’s reactio</p>
  <ol>Reinforcement</ol>
  <p>A consequence that increases the likelihood of a behaviour happening again.</p>
  <ol>Punishment</ol>
  <p>A consequence intended to reduce a behaviour; it is not the same as negative reinforcement.</p>
  
  
  
